<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/expert/generative-grammar">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Generative grammar is <strong>Chomsky's programme: language as a formal computational system
    with built-in structure</strong> — the most influential, productive and controversial theory
    in linguistics. It formalised syntax (the trees lesson), launched the cognitive revolution,
    and set the terms every rival theory still answers.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from Syntactic Structures to Minimalism — six decades of the programme" widget="equationStep" config={genCfg} />

  <h2>The arguments, both sides</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For generativism:</strong> poverty-of-stimulus, species-universality, acquisition speed, creole genesis, modality-independence (sign languages acquire identically) — the evidence pattern fits built-in structure.</li>
    <li><strong>Against / alternatives:</strong> construction grammar (usage-based learning of form-meaning pairs), cognitive grammar (meaning first), emergentist accounts (general learning mechanisms suffice) — the "usage-based" programme is generativism's serious rival.</li>
    <li><strong>The 2019 Nobelprijzen-adjacent test case:</strong> deep language models (LLMs) learn grammar from raw text without built-in grammar — a live experiment on the poverty-of-stimulus claim. Both camps claim the result; the argument continues with new evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>What survives regardless:</strong> formalisation of syntax, tacit knowledge as object of study, the competence/performance distinction, and the generative-transformational machinery that every parser and formal semanticist uses.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chomsky, Syntactic Structures (1957) and Aspects (1965); Minimalist Program (1995); Pullum & Scholz on the poverty-of-stimulus debate.</p>
</TopicPage>
